DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     A linker takes one or more object, archive, and library files, and com‐
     bines them into an output file (an executable, a shared library, or an‐
     other object file).  It relocates code and data from the input files and
     resolves symbol references between them.

     lldd..lllldd is a drop-in replacement for the GNU BFD and gold linkers.  It ac‐
     cepts most of the same command line arguments and linker scripts as GNU
     linkers.

     lldd..lllldd currently supports i386, x86-64, ARM, AArch64, LoongArch, Pow‐
     erPC32, PowerPC64, MIPS32, MIPS64, RISC-V, AMDGPU, Hexagon and SPARC V9
     targets.  lldd..lllldd acts as a Microsoft link.exe-compatible linker if in‐
     voked as lllldd--lliinnkk and as macOS's ld if invoked as lldd..lldd6644.. All these tar‐
     gets are always supported however lldd..lllldd was built, so you can always use
     lldd..lllldd as a native linker as well as a cross linker.

